<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Leetspeak</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Leetspeak is a form of coded language where letters are replaced with numbers or symbols, and is commonly used in online communities.
Since Leetspeak in not a standard encoding method, prompts may have different encoding styles. In this paper, when we refer to Leetspeak, we use the
mapping shown in Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.1 Leetspeak ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Broken-Token: Filtering Obfuscated Prompts by Counting Characters-Per-Token" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
